MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m07",
  "summary": "Explain diesel combustion and how diesel fuel is supplied and injected, including common-rail systems, glow plugs and turbochargers, and diagnose diesel faults safely.",
  "outcomes": ["Explain compression ignition and how a diesel engine differs from a petrol engine.","Describe the diesel fuel supply system, water in fuel, priming and cold-weather problems.","Describe the main injection systems and how a common-rail system works.","Explain how glow plugs help cold starting and how to test them.","Describe how a turbocharger and intercooler work and how to look after them.","Diagnose hard starting, smoke, loss of power and knock, and work safely around high-pressure fuel."],
  "cheatsheet": ["A diesel ignites its fuel by compression: no spark plugs, high compression ratio (about 15:1 to 23:1).","Cetane number measures how readily diesel ignites. Higher cetane means smoother starting and burning.","Black smoke = too much fuel or too little air. Blue = oil burning. White = unburnt fuel or coolant.","Water in diesel causes corrosion and injector damage. Drain the water separator regularly.","After a fuel filter change or running out of fuel, prime and bleed the system before cranking.","Common rail: a high-pressure pump fills a rail, and the computer opens injectors electrically.","Glow plugs heat the combustion chamber so a cold engine can start.","A turbocharger spins at very high speed on engine oil. Oil starvation destroys it quickly.","Never check for a high-pressure diesel leak with your hand. Spray can penetrate the skin."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m07-l1", "title": "Diesel combustion", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A diesel engine looks like a petrol engine but burns its fuel in a different way. Understanding the difference explains most of what is special about diesel engines."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression ignition"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a petrol engine a spark ignites a mixture of air and fuel. In a diesel engine, only air enters the cylinder on the intake stroke. On the compression stroke the piston squeezes the air to a very high pressure, and the air becomes hot, about 500 °C or more. Near the top of the stroke, fuel is injected as a fine spray. It meets the hot air and ignites by itself, which is called **compression ignition**. There are no spark plugs."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression ratio"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Petrol engines usually have compression ratios between about 9:1 and 13:1. Diesel engines need much more, typically from about 15:1 up to 23:1. This is why diesel engines have strong, heavy blocks, crankshafts and pistons to withstand the higher pressures."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Feature","Direct injection (DI)","Indirect injection (IDI)"],"rows":[["Where fuel is sprayed","Straight into a shaped bowl in the piston crown","Into a small pre-chamber or swirl chamber in the head"],["Efficiency","Higher. Less heat is lost.","Lower"],["Noise","Louder, with a sharp diesel knock","Quieter and smoother"],["Cold starting","Harder, needs good heating","Easier, with glow plugs in the pre-chamber"],["Where you find it","Almost all modern engines","Older cars and light trucks"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why diesels are efficient"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The high compression ratio extracts more work from each unit of fuel.","The engine is not throttled in the same way, so it does not waste energy drawing air past a nearly closed throttle at light load.","Diesel fuel contains more energy per litre than petrol.","The engine produces high torque at low speed, so it suits heavy loads."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cetane in plain terms"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **cetane number** of diesel fuel measures how quickly it ignites after it is injected, so a higher number means better ignition. A fuel with a low cetane number makes the engine hard to start, noisy and smoky. It is the diesel equivalent of the octane rating for petrol, but works in the opposite way: octane resists ignition, and cetane promotes it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Smoke as a clue"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Smoke colour","Common cause"],"rows":[["Black","Too much fuel for the air: blocked air filter, faulty injector, turbo fault, overloaded engine"],["Blue","Engine oil burning: worn rings, valve seals, or a turbocharger oil leak"],["White","Unburnt fuel on a cold start or with low compression and late injection. Also coolant if a head gasket has failed"]],"caption":"Diesel smoke colours"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine characteristics"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Diesel engines usually run at lower speeds than petrol engines. A typical car diesel peaks at 4,000 to 5,000 rpm, and a truck diesel at 2,000 to 3,000 rpm.","They have a long stroke and high torque from low speed, so they pull well without much gearbox work.","The fuel itself lubricates the injection pump. Poor, low-lubricity fuel wears the pump quickly.","Diesels burn with a large excess of air, so lambda is always greater than 1.0, and the exhaust has plenty of oxygen but a lot of soot and nitrogen oxides."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A diesel engine controls its power by changing the amount of fuel injected, not the amount of air. That is why fuel delivery is so important and so carefully controlled."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A smoky diesel is a diagnostic gift. Note the colour, when it appears (cold, under load, at start) and report it on the job card."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l2", "title": "Diesel combustion phases, timing and emissions", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Burning diesel fuel in a hot cylinder is more complicated than lighting a premixed charge with a spark. The fuel is sprayed in while the piston is rising, and it mixes and burns in a few thousandths of a second. This lesson explains how combustion progresses, how the injection timing changes the result, and why diesel engines have the emission problems they do."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The phases of combustion"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Ignition delay.** The first fuel injected needs a short time to evaporate, mix with the air and reach its ignition temperature. This delay is a fraction of a millisecond. Fuel with a higher cetane number ignites sooner, giving a shorter delay.","**Premixed burning.** The fuel injected during the delay burns all at once. This causes a rapid pressure rise, the typical diesel knock. A longer delay means more fuel collects before burning, so the knock is sharper and the noise louder.","**Diffusion burning.** Fuel injected after ignition burns as it mixes with the air. This phase is controlled by how quickly fuel mixes, and it produces most of the power and most of the soot.","**Late burning.** The last of the fuel and soot burns as the piston moves down. Poor mixing here leaves soot in the exhaust."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Injection timing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Injecting earlier gives the fuel more time to mix before ignition, which raises peak pressure and temperature. This lifts power and efficiency a little, but it increases the formation of nitrogen oxides (NOx) and combustion noise. Injecting later lowers NOx but makes soot, white smoke and fuel use go up, and exhaust temperatures rise. The computer sets the timing from engine speed, load and temperature and shifts it to meet emission limits."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The NOx and soot trade-off"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","NOx","Soot"],"rows":[["Early injection, high temperature","High","Low"],["Late injection, lower temperature","Low","High"],["Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) added","Lower, because the charge is cooler","Higher, because there is less oxygen"],["Higher injection pressure","Slightly higher","Lower, because fuel mixes better"]],"caption":"Engineers use EGR, high pressure and multiple injections together to reduce both. Module 8 covers the exhaust treatment that completes the job."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why smoke limits power"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A diesel engine is always run with excess air, but if too much fuel is injected for the air available, the extra burns poorly and makes black smoke. The maximum fuel at each speed is therefore limited by the **smoke limit**. Raising the fuel quantity above it, by tuning or a faulty injector, gives more smoke, higher exhaust temperatures and risk of turbo and piston damage. A boost leak lowers the air and reduces the smoke limit, so the engine is down on power and smokes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diesel knock and noise"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The diesel knock is a normal sound, but it should not be a harsh clatter. Causes of a louder knock are a low cetane fuel, late or early timing, a faulty injector, low compression and a cold engine. Pilot injection reduces the knock by starting a gentle burn before the main injection."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"In a diesel, the air quantity limits how much fuel can be burned cleanly. Anything that reduces the air, a blocked filter, a boost leak or a failing turbo, shows up as smoke and loss of power."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When a diesel smokes, think about air first, then fuel. Check the intake and boost before you condemn the injectors."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l3", "title": "Diesel fuel supply", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Diesel injectors and pumps are made with extremely tight tolerances, so clean fuel supply is vital. This lesson follows diesel fuel from the tank to the injection system."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Fuel tank:** holds the diesel. Water and dirt settle at the bottom.","**Lift pump (feed pump):** an electric or mechanical pump that draws fuel from the tank and feeds it to the injection pump at low pressure.","**Fuel filter with water separator:** removes fine particles and separates water. Many have a drain valve for the water and a water-in-fuel warning light.","**Priming pump:** a hand-operated pump or a bulb used to fill the system with fuel after a service or running out of fuel.","**High-pressure pump and injectors:** raise the pressure and spray the fuel into the cylinders."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Priming and bleeding"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Air in the fuel system stops a diesel from starting, because air can be compressed by the pump and will not push fuel through the injectors. After changing a filter, repairing a leak or running the tank dry, the air must be removed. Follow the manual: loosen the bleed screw, operate the primer until fuel flows without bubbles, and tighten. Some modern systems prime themselves when the ignition is cycled several times."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check there is fuel in the tank, and the filter is fitted correctly and full.","Open the bleed screw on the filter housing or the injection pump as the manual shows, with a rag under it.","Operate the hand primer until clear fuel flows from the screw without any bubbles, then close the screw while still pumping.","If the manual calls for it, bleed the high-pressure line at the injector by loosening the connection while cranking, and tighten as soon as fuel appears. Do this only on systems where the manual allows it. Never loosen common-rail lines.","Start the engine. If it fails to fire in 15 seconds, stop and bleed again, to save the starter and battery."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Water and contamination"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Water in diesel causes rust, corrosion of pump and injector surfaces, and poor combustion. In warm, damp conditions, **microbial growth** (a slimy brown or black growth from bacteria and fungi) can form where water and fuel meet. It blocks filters. Keep tanks full to limit condensation, drain the water separator at the interval in the schedule, and use fuel from a reliable source."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cold weather"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In very cold weather, wax crystals form in diesel and can block the filter. This is called waxing. Winter-grade diesel resists it. Many vehicles have a **fuel heater** at the filter to prevent waxing. Using petrol or kerosene in diesel is not safe unless the manufacturer allows it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Biodiesel basics"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Biodiesel** is made from vegetable oils or fats and is blended with diesel, as in B7 or B20 (7% or 20% biodiesel). It is a renewable fuel but attracts water and can degrade rubber seals and filters on older vehicles. Check the manual for the blends the engine allows."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel specifications"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Property","What it means","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Cetane number","Ignition quality. Usually 45 to 55.","Low cetane gives hard starting, noise and smoke"],["Sulphur content","Low or ultra-low sulphur diesel has very little sulphur","High sulphur harms exhaust treatment systems and oil, and it makes acid"],["Lubricity","How well the fuel protects pump parts","Ultra-low sulphur fuel needs additives to keep lubricity"],["Cloud point and cold filter plugging","Temperatures at which wax forms and blocks the filter","Winter fuel is blended for the local climate"],["Water content","Should be very low","Water causes rust, microbial growth and injector damage"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"High-pressure systems are far more sensitive than old pumps. A few grams of dirt or a teaspoon of water can ruin a common-rail pump or injector costing hundreds of times as much. In regions where fuel quality is variable, use a reliable source, change filters on time and drain the water separator regularly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Diesel fuel is less volatile than petrol but still flammable. Keep it away from flames and wipe up spills, which also make floors slippery."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a diesel starts and then dies, or is hard to start after a filter change, suspect air in the fuel before anything else."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l4", "title": "Injection systems", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The injection system has to deliver the correct quantity of fuel at exactly the right time, at very high pressure. This lesson gives you an outline of the main types and a closer look at common-rail systems."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Older mechanical systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Older diesel engines used an **in-line pump**, with one pumping element for each cylinder, or a **rotary (distributor) pump**, with a single pumping element and a rotating distributor. A mechanical governor controlled the engine speed. These pumps are driven by the engine and send fuel through high-pressure pipes to injectors that open when the pressure is high enough."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Unit injectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **unit injector** combines the pump and the injector in one unit for each cylinder, driven by the camshaft. This avoids long high-pressure pipes and allows very high pressure."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common rail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"In a **common-rail** system, a high-pressure pump raises the diesel pressure to a very high level, often well over 1,000 bar and in modern systems up to about 2,000 bar or more. The fuel is stored in a shared pipe called the **rail**. A rail pressure sensor reports the pressure to the engine computer, and a pressure control valve keeps it at the target value. Each injector is opened electrically by the computer, which controls the timing and duration of injection."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"common-rail-diesel"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Multiple injection events"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Because the pressure is already available, the computer can inject fuel several times per cycle. A small **pilot injection** before the main injection softens the start of burning, which reduces noise. The **main injection** produces the power. A **post injection** after the main one can help to clean up emissions. This gives quiet running, good economy and low emissions."},
      {"t":"table","head":["System","How pressure is made","Typical feature"],"rows":[["In-line pump","Pumping element for each cylinder","Robust, older vehicles and trucks"],["Rotary pump","Single element and distributor","Compact, mechanical"],["Unit injector","Cam-driven pump in each injector","High pressure, no long pipes"],["Common rail","One pump, shared rail, electronic injectors","Flexible timing, quiet, low emissions"]],"caption":"Diesel injection systems"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical injection pressures"},
      {"t":"table","head":["System","Typical injection pressure"],"rows":[["Indirect injection, mechanical","About 120 to 150 bar"],["Direct injection, in-line or rotary pump","About 200 to 700 bar"],["Unit injector or unit pump","About 1,000 to 2,000 bar"],["Common rail","About 1,350 to 2,500 bar or more"]],"caption":"Higher pressure gives finer droplets, better mixing, cleaner burning and quieter running."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Solenoid and piezo injectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **solenoid injector** has a coil that lifts a control valve, which releases the pressure above the nozzle needle and lets the system pressure lift it. A **piezo injector** uses a stack of crystals that change length when a voltage is applied. They react much faster than solenoids, allowing more injections per cycle and finer control. Both work at high voltage and pressure, and neither should be tested with 12 V or compressed air. Use the scan tool and the manual."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Common-rail pressure is so high that it can penetrate skin. Never loosen a high-pressure line or fitting when the engine is running or has just been run. Wait for the pressure to fall as the manual directs."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Injectors in a common-rail system are precision parts. Always keep them clean, do not mix their positions, and code a replacement injector to the computer if the manual requires it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l5", "title": "Mechanical injection pumps and nozzles", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Many diesel vehicles on the road, especially trucks, tractors, generators and older cars and used imports, have mechanical injection pumps, and common-rail technicians still need to understand them. This lesson explains how the in-line and rotary pumps work, how the nozzle is built and tested, and what is safe to adjust."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The in-line pump"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An in-line pump has one plunger and barrel (element) for each cylinder. The camshaft in the pump lifts each plunger in turn. A spiral groove, or **helix**, cut in the plunger controls the quantity: turning the plunger with a toothed rack changes the point at which the helix uncovers a port and ends the delivery. A **delivery valve** at the top holds the pressure in the line between strokes and cuts off injection sharply. A **governor** on the pump moves the rack to keep the engine at idle and prevent over-speeding."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The rotary (distributor) pump"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A rotary pump has a single pumping element. A rotating **distributor head** sends fuel to each outlet in turn. Opposed plungers are pushed together by a cam ring, and the distance they move sets the quantity. A **vane-type transfer pump** supplies fuel, and a **timing device**, a piston moved by fuel pressure, advances the injection with speed. A stop solenoid cuts off the fuel when the ignition is switched off. A fault in the solenoid or its wiring stops the engine from starting or from shutting off."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Pump","Strength","Weakness"],"rows":[["In-line","Strong, long-lasting, high flow for large engines","Big and heavy"],["Rotary","Small and light, for car engines","Wears faster with poor fuel, and is sensitive to air and dirt"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Injector nozzles"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A mechanical injector holds a needle valve against its seat with a spring. When the fuel pressure rises above the **opening pressure** (pop pressure), the needle lifts and fuel sprays out. A **hole-type nozzle** has several small holes and is used for direct injection. A **pintle nozzle** has a pin that shapes the spray and is used on indirect injection engines. The nozzle should spray a fine, even mist and shut cleanly with no dribble."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing nozzles"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Mount the injector on a nozzle tester. Wear eye protection and keep your hands clear of the spray. A spray at injection pressure can penetrate skin.","Pump the lever slowly and watch the gauge at the moment the needle lifts. Compare the opening pressure with the manual. Typical values are about 120 to 300 bar depending on the nozzle type.","Check the spray pattern. It should be even, fine and symmetric from each hole, with a slight chatter at slow pumping.","Hold the pressure just below opening and watch the nozzle tip for dripping for ten seconds. A drip means a worn seat.","Adjust the opening pressure by shims or the screw, if the nozzle is adjustable, and then recheck it."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What not to touch"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Governor screws, maximum speed stops and fuel delivery limits are sealed by the maker. Tampering raises fuel delivery and engine speed beyond safe limits, causing smoke, overheating and mechanical damage, and may break the law. If an engine needs adjustment, do it in line with the manual and replace the seals, or send the pump to a specialist with a test bench."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Timing the pump"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The pump is timed to the engine so that injection begins at a precise angle before top dead centre. The pump drive has marks, and a mark on the engine and the pump flange show the setting. Fit the pump with the engine at the timing position, and adjust the pump flange slightly to set the **static timing** exactly, as the manual says. A pump that is over-advanced makes a harsh knock and may overheat. A pump that is retarded gives white smoke, poor power and difficult starting."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never test a nozzle with your hand or finger near the spray. Fuel injected into the skin at high pressure causes serious injury, and needs urgent hospital treatment."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Mechanical pumps are tough but sensitive to dirt, air and water. Most failures come from poor fuel and neglected filters, not wear."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l6", "title": "Glow plugs and cold starting", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A cold diesel engine has trouble heating the compressed air enough to ignite the fuel, because the cold metal soaks up the heat. Starting aids solve this problem."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Glow plugs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **glow plug** is a small heating element that screws into the combustion chamber of each cylinder, or into a pre-chamber on some engines. When it is switched on, it heats to a high temperature quickly and warms the air and the chamber. A dashboard light tells the driver to wait until the plugs have finished heating before starting. After the engine starts, glow plugs may keep running for a short time to reduce smoke and noise."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **glow plug relay** or control module switches the plugs. The computer decides how long they run according to the coolant temperature. Newer ceramic or fast-heating plugs can reach glowing temperature in a few seconds. In warm weather the plugs may not be used at all."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Glow plug type","How it works","Notes"],"rows":[["Sheathed (metal)","A heating coil inside a metal tube","Heats in about 5 to 10 seconds. Common on older engines."],["Ceramic or fast-start","A ceramic heating element","Heats in 2 seconds or less. Stays hot after the engine starts."],["Pressure sensor glow plug","A glow plug with a built-in cylinder pressure sensor","Used on a few engines. Handle with great care."]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some engines also use an **intake air heater**, a heating grid in the intake manifold, or a **coolant heater**. A **block heater**, an electric element in the cooling system that is plugged into the mains, is used in very cold climates to warm the engine before starting. Never run a block heater with a low coolant level."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing glow plugs"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Disconnect the battery and the connections to the glow plugs.","Measure the resistance of each plug with a multimeter between the terminal and the engine. A good plug typically has a low resistance, of a few ohms or less. Check the manual for the value.","An open circuit (infinite resistance) means the plug has failed.","Alternatively, measure the current drawn by each plug with a clamp meter when the system is activated. A plug that draws no current is faulty."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Likely result"],"rows":[["One plug failed","Rough running and white smoke for the first minute on a cold start"],["Several plugs failed","Hard starting when cold, heavy white smoke"],["Relay or control fault","None of the plugs heat, so hard starting in cold weather"]],"caption":"Glow plug faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other hard-starting causes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Hard starting can also be caused by a weak battery, air in the fuel, a blocked filter, low compression, worn injectors or low-quality fuel. If the engine starts easily when warm but not when cold, suspect the glow plug system."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Why compression heats the air"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When air is compressed quickly, it has no time to lose heat, and its temperature rises. For this, an approximate formula is used, with temperatures in kelvin (add 273 to the Celsius value):"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"T2 = T1 × (compression ratio) ^ 0.4","caption":"Temperatures in kelvin. The exponent 0.4 is 1.4 minus 1, for air."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Why a cold diesel is hard to start","steps":["The engine has a compression ratio of 18:1. On a warm day the intake air is 20 °C, which is 293 K.","18 raised to 0.4 is about 3.18, so T2 = 293 × 3.18 = 932 K, which is about 659 °C. Real engines reach less than this because heat is lost to the walls, but the air is far hotter than diesel's ignition temperature of about 250 to 350 °C.","In cold weather the intake air is −20 °C, which is 253 K. T2 = 253 × 3.18 = 804 K, about 531 °C. The cold metal absorbs much more heat and the engine turns slowly, so the real temperature is well below this and may not reach the ignition point.","Glow plugs and heaters add the missing heat. This is why diesels with weak batteries and failed plugs struggle most on cold mornings."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use starting fluid (ether) in an engine with glow plugs or an air-intake heater. It can ignite violently and cause serious engine and personal damage."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a glow plug will not come out, warm the engine slightly and use penetrating oil. Pull gently with the right-sized socket, because a plug that snaps in the head is a very costly job to remove."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l7", "title": "Turbocharging and intercooling", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most modern diesel engines are turbocharged. A turbocharger lets a small engine produce the power of a larger one, and it improves economy and emissions. This lesson explains how it works and how to look after it."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"turbocharger"},
      {"t":"h","text":"How it works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **turbocharger** has two wheels joined by a shaft. The hot exhaust gas spins the **turbine wheel**. The turbine shaft turns the **compressor wheel** on the intake side, which draws in air and forces it into the engine at a pressure above atmospheric. This extra pressure is called **boost pressure**. More air allows more fuel to be burned, so more power is made. Turbochargers spin at very high speed, often over 100,000 rpm."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Controlling the boost"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **wastegate** is a valve that lets some exhaust gas bypass the turbine, which limits the boost.","A **variable geometry turbocharger (VGT)** has moving vanes that change the gas flow, to give good boost at low and high speed. The computer controls the vanes.","A **boost pressure sensor** reports the pressure to the computer."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Actuator type","How it works","Faults"],"rows":[["Vacuum actuator","A vacuum capsule moves the wastegate or vanes, controlled by a solenoid","Cracked vacuum hose, leaking diaphragm, stuck linkage"],["Electric actuator","An electric motor with a position sensor moves the vanes","Needs recalibration after replacement, sensor faults, stuck linkage"],["Pressure-fed (mechanical)","Boost pressure itself pushes a diaphragm to open the wastegate","Leaking or stuck diaphragm gives over-boost or low boost"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Boost pressure is typically 1.0 to 2.0 bar above atmospheric on a car diesel. Boost control faults cause low power and limp mode (too little boost), or over-boost, which can damage the engine, and the computer cuts fuel when it senses it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Intercooler"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Compressing air heats it, and hot air is less dense. An **intercooler** is a radiator that cools the compressed air before it enters the engine. Cooler air is denser, so more oxygen enters the cylinder, and combustion temperatures are lower."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil and care"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The turbocharger shaft spins on bearings lubricated by engine oil, and many are also cooled by engine coolant. Oil starvation destroys a turbo within seconds. Always use the correct oil, change it on time, fill the turbo with oil or prime it after replacing it, and do not rev the engine as soon as it starts. Some manufacturers advise letting a hot engine idle for a short while before switching off, so the turbo can cool. Follow the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Faults"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Loss of power, low boost","Leak in the intake pipes or intercooler, stuck wastegate or vanes, damaged turbo"],["Blue smoke","Turbo oil seal leaking into the intake or exhaust"],["Whining or siren noise","Worn bearings or a leak in the boost pipe"],["Black smoke","Boost leak means too little air for the fuel"]],"caption":"Turbocharger faults"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing for a boost leak"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the intercooler and every pipe and clamp for oil, splits, cracks and loose clips. Oil on the outside of a joint shows where pressure escapes.","Disconnect the intake pipe at the turbo and look for oil in the pipe, play in the compressor wheel and damage to the vanes.","Pressure-test the intake side using an adapter and a regulated air supply, at a low pressure (about 0.5 bar) as the manual allows, and listen or use soapy water on the joints to find leaks.","Compare the actual boost against the desired boost on a scan tool during a road test, with a second person watching or logging the data."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing a turbocharger"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Find out why the old one failed. If oil was starved, blocked or contaminated, the new one will fail too.","Clean or replace the oil feed pipe and make sure the drain is free and not blocked or kinked.","Change the oil and filter, and clean out any debris or metal from the intake and intercooler.","Prime the turbo with clean oil before fitting, and crank the engine without starting it until oil pressure is seen, as the manual advises.","Run the engine at idle for a short time after starting and do not rev it hard until the turbo is lubricated."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Failure symptom","Typical cause"],"rows":[["Oil on the compressor wheel and in the intercooler","Blocked drain or crankcase breather, or a worn seal"],["Blades bent or chipped","Debris from the air intake or from a broken engine part"],["Blue-black bearing area, burnt oil","Starved of oil or very hot shutdown"],["Sharp scoring on the shaft and bearings","Dirty oil"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most turbo failures are caused by something else: dirty or low oil, a blocked oil feed pipe, debris from the engine or intake. Find the cause before you fit a new turbo."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l8", "title": "Diesel diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson brings the previous five together into a diagnostic approach for the common diesel complaints, with a strong focus on safety."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hard starting or no start"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the battery and starter. The engine must crank at normal speed.","Check for fuel in the tank and air in the system. Prime and bleed if necessary.","Check the filter and water separator for blockage or water.","Check the glow plug system if the engine is cold.","Read any fault codes. Common-rail engines have many sensors that can prevent starting, such as the rail pressure sensor and the crankshaft sensor.","Check compression if everything else is good."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Smoke"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use the smoke colour and the conditions as a guide. Black smoke under load is usually too little air or too much fuel: check the air filter, the intake pipes for leaks, the turbo and the injectors. White smoke when cold may be a glow plug fault or low compression. Blue smoke is oil: check for worn rings, turbo seals or an overfilled sump."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Loss of power"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check for a blocked fuel filter, a blocked air filter, a boost leak, a blocked exhaust or particulate filter (see module 8), and low fuel pressure. Many modern engines go into **limp mode** when the computer detects a fault, which limits power. A scan tool will show the reason."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Knock and injectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Diesel engines are noisier than petrol engines, but a loud knock that varies with speed can mean an injector problem or incorrect timing. An injector that sticks open can wash oil off the cylinder wall and cause damage. A **return flow test** measures how much fuel each injector returns to the tank in a set time. A high return flow means the injector is worn and leaking internally. Follow the manual's procedure and specification."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel contamination"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If the wrong fuel has been put into the tank, for example petrol in a diesel, do not start the engine. Drain the tank and lines, replace the filters and prime the system. Running the engine can destroy the high-pressure pump and injectors."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Loss of power on a hill.** A diesel pick-up loses power when climbing, with no smoke. The scan tool shows desired boost 1.6 bar and actual 0.9 bar. Smoke testing the intake finds a split in a rubber boost hose beside the intercooler. Fitting a new hose restores boost, and the engine pulls normally."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Hard start, rough for the first minute.** A diesel starts well when warm, but is hard to start when cold, and runs unevenly with white smoke for about a minute. A clamp meter shows that only three of four glow plugs draw current. The fourth plug is open circuit. A set of new glow plugs cures it."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Black smoke and poor fuel economy.** A diesel van produces black smoke under load. The air filter is clean and boost is normal. The return flow test shows that one injector returns three times as much fuel as the others, so it leaks internally and floods that cylinder. The injector is replaced and coded to the engine computer."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","First checks"],"rows":[["Will not crank","Battery, starter, immobiliser, earth cables"],["Cranks but no start","Fuel in the tank, air in the system, filter, rail pressure, glow plugs, crank sensor"],["Starts then stalls","Air in the fuel, blocked filter, failing lift pump, leaking intake"],["Low power or limp mode","Fault codes, boost leaks, blocked filters, particulate filter, injectors"],["Loud knocking","Injector timing, a sticking injector, a bearing"],["Excess white smoke","Glow plugs, low compression, coolant leak"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never check for a high-pressure diesel leak by running your hand along the pipes. A pinhole leak can inject fuel through the skin, causing serious tissue damage and infection, and needs urgent medical help. Use a piece of cardboard held at a safe distance, with eye protection, or follow the manual's leak-check procedure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Always read the fault codes before you start. Many diesel faults leave a clear code that points to the circuit, and it saves a lot of guessing."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l9", "title": "Common-rail systems and diagnosis in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Common-rail diesel is now the standard on cars, vans and many trucks. It is precise and efficient, but it is also unforgiving of dirt, air and wrong procedures. This lesson explains the layout and shows how to diagnose faults and handle the parts without damaging them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The low-pressure circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An electric or mechanical **lift pump** pulls fuel from the tank through the filter at a few bar and delivers it to the high-pressure pump. The filter and water separator protect the pump and may have a heater and a water sensor. A restriction or an air leak in this low-pressure side is a common cause of hard starting, stalling and power loss, so inspect it first. A **fuel temperature sensor** helps the computer correct the fuel density."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The high-pressure pump and rail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A radial-piston or in-line pump driven by the engine raises the pressure. A **metering valve** (suction control valve) at the pump inlet controls how much fuel is allowed in, so the pump compresses only what is needed. A **pressure control valve** on the rail releases excess pressure back to the tank. The **rail pressure sensor** gives the computer the real pressure, which it compares with the target."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Condition","Typical rail pressure"],"rows":[["Cranking","At least 200 to 300 bar needed to start"],["Idle, warm engine","About 250 to 450 bar"],["Full load","1,500 to 2,500 bar or more"]],"caption":"Values vary by engine. Use the specification for the vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Injectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Each injector is a precise valve. It has a **leak-off** (return) line that takes the small amount of control fuel back to the tank. Many have a **correction code** printed or etched on the body, which the computer uses to adjust that injector's quantity. A replacement injector must be **coded** into the computer with the scan tool, or the engine will run unevenly and may set codes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnosing with a scan tool"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Compare **actual rail pressure** with **desired rail pressure** at idle and under load. A big gap indicates a weak pump, a stuck valve, a leak or a bad sensor.","Read the **injector correction values** or the **cylinder balance** (idle speed contribution). A cylinder with a large correction has a worn or leaking injector.","Check the **metering valve duty cycle**. A very high duty cycle shows that the pump is working hard because of a restriction, leak or worn pump.","Watch for **fuel temperature** and **rail pressure** when cranking. If the pressure does not reach about 200 bar, the engine will not start.","Run the manufacturer's tests, such as the **injector leak test** and the **pump delivery test**, from the scan tool menu."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Cranks but no start, rail pressure low","Air in the low-pressure side, weak lift pump, blocked filter, failed high-pressure pump or stuck metering valve"],["Starts then stalls, pressure falls","Leaking injector, pressure control valve open, rail leak"],["Engine misfires on one cylinder, high return flow","Worn or stuck injector"],["Power loss and limp mode with rail pressure code","Metering valve, sensor, low fuel supply, or worn pump"],["Metal in the filter","Pump failure, which spreads debris through the whole system"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cleanliness rules"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Clean around every connection before you open it. Even a speck of dust can block a nozzle.","Cap every open line, pump and injector at once with the correct plugs and keep them in a clean place.","Use only clean, lint-free cloths and clean tools. Never use compressed air near an open fuel system.","Do not turn the engine over with fuel lines off or injectors out, except as the manual instructs.","After a pump or injector failure that sheds metal, the whole system must be flushed, and the lines, rail and filter renewed, or the new parts will be damaged straight away."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handling and refitting injectors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Remove injectors with the correct tool and note their positions. Do not drop them or knock the nozzle. Replace the copper sealing washers, and the seals on the return line, and refit the clamps and torque exactly as in the manual, because uneven tightening distorts the injector. Never loosen a pipe on a running engine. After refitting, prime the fuel system, check the codes and run a test. Code any new injector to the engine computer."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never loosen or remove a high-pressure fuel line, an injector or a rail connection while the engine is running or has recently run. Wait the time the manual gives for the pressure to fall. A pinhole leak can inject fuel through the skin."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"In a common-rail system, 90% of the faults are air, dirt, wiring or low-pressure supply. Check those before you condemn an expensive pump or injector."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m07-l10", "title": "Diesel engine service, checks and runaway", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A diesel engine is a long-lived machine if it is serviced properly, and an expensive problem if it is not. This lesson covers the points that make diesel servicing different from petrol: oil, filters, compression testing and a rare but serious danger, engine runaway."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil and filters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Diesel oil collects more soot, and the oil turns black soon after a change. This is normal. Soot, acids and fuel dilution still wear the oil out, so diesels often need shorter intervals in dusty or short-trip use. Use oil of the specification in the manual, with the right level of ash content if the engine has a particulate filter (module 8). Replace the air filter more often in dusty places, because a diesel engine takes in a great deal of air. Replace the fuel filter at the schedule interval, and drain the water separator at each oil change."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Compression testing a diesel"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Warm the engine, disable the fuel supply or injector fuse so that it does not start, and remove the glow plugs or injectors, according to the manual.","Fit a **diesel compression tester** of the correct range and adapter into each hole in turn. A petrol tester has too low a range.","Crank the engine for about five seconds with a healthy battery, and record the highest reading.","A healthy diesel typically reads about 25 to 40 bar. Compare with the manual. The cylinders should agree within about 10% of each other.","Refit the glow plugs or injectors with new seals, tightened to the torque in the manual."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other checks specific to diesels"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Crankcase breather:** a blocked breather gives oil leaks and smoke. The oil mist can also block the intake and EGR.","**Intake system:** oily deposits in the intake and intercooler point to a worn turbo or a blocked breather.","**Cooling:** diesels run warm and carry high loads. Check coolant, belts and fans carefully.","**Mounts:** diesels vibrate more. Worn mounts and a worn dual-mass flywheel give noise and shake.","**Timing belt or chain:** follow the schedule strictly, since many diesels are interference engines."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diesel runaway"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A diesel can, in rare cases, run away: it speeds up out of control because it is burning something other than the fuel the computer is injecting, usually its own engine oil drawn in through a failed turbo seal, an overfilled sump or a leaking breather. Because a diesel needs no spark, switching off the ignition or cutting the fuel does not stop it. It keeps running on the oil, and can rev past the engine's limit and wreck itself."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Warning sign","What to do"],"rows":[["Engine speed rises on its own and does not respond to the pedal","Do not stand in front of the engine or its exhaust. Keep others away."],["Thick white or blue smoke and a rising roar","The only way to stop it is to cut the air. If it is safe to do so, block the air intake with a thick flat object, or use a carbon dioxide extinguisher into the intake. Never put your hands near the intake."],["Vehicle is on the road","Select a high gear, apply the brakes firmly and stall the engine if you can, or switch off the ignition and wait for the oil to be used up. Call for help."]],"caption":"Prevention is better than cure: repair turbo leaks, check the oil level (never overfill) and clear a blocked breather."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Never ignore an unexplained rise in oil level, white or blue smoke at idle or oil in the intake. They are warnings that precede runaway and engine failure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"If a diesel runs away, keep clear. Do not stand in front of the intake or in line with the exhaust, and never try to stop it with your hands or a cloth."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Prime and bleed a diesel system and test glow plugs","objective":"Prime and bleed the fuel system of a training diesel engine after a filter change, and measure the resistance of each glow plug.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["A diesel training engine with a hand primer or bleed screw","A new fuel filter, a drain container and rags","Multimeter and a clamp meter","Socket set and torque wrench","Workshop manual with glow plug specifications"],"safety":["Do not loosen any high-pressure fuel line on a running or recently run engine.","Catch spilled diesel and wipe the floor, as diesel is slippery.","Keep the engine off while you bleed the system, and keep ignition sources away.","Disconnect the battery before you disconnect the glow plug wiring.","Wear gloves and safety glasses, and dispose of old filters and fuel correctly."],"steps":["Find the filter position, bleed screw and primer position in the manual.","Place the drain container and rags below the filter housing.","Remove the old filter, noting any water or dirt in the filter housing.","Fit the new filter with a lightly oiled seal and tighten as the manual directs.","Open the bleed screw and pump the primer until fuel flows without bubbles.","Tighten the bleed screw and pump the primer until it feels firm.","Start the engine, allow it to run and check for leaks at the filter.","Switch off and disconnect the battery. Disconnect the glow plug wiring.","Measure the resistance of each glow plug with the meter and record the values.","Compare each reading with the specification and mark any plug that is open or out of range.","Reconnect the wiring and battery and confirm the glow plug warning light behaves correctly.","Dispose of the old filter and fuel correctly and clean the work area."],"checks":["I primed and bled the system until fuel flowed without air bubbles.","I checked for leaks after the engine started.","I measured each glow plug and compared the reading with the specification.","I did not loosen any high-pressure line on a running engine.","I disposed of fuel and filters correctly."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m07-q01","q":"How does a diesel engine ignite its fuel?","options":["A glow plug stays on and sets light to the fuel every time it is injected","A spark plug ignites the fuel when the piston reaches the top of its stroke","The starter motor heats the fuel in the injector before it is sprayed in","The air is compressed until it is hot enough to ignite the injected fuel"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Compression ignition needs no spark. The temperature of the compressed air ignites the fuel."},
    {"id":"m07-q02","q":"Why must a diesel engine have a stronger construction than a petrol engine?","options":["It uses a heavier fuel that needs much larger pistons and bigger bearings","It works at a much higher compression ratio and cylinder pressure","It runs at a much higher engine speed than a petrol engine does","It has more cylinders than a petrol engine of the same overall power"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Compression ratios of up to 23:1 produce very high pressures, so the parts must be strong."},
    {"id":"m07-q03","q":"What does a higher cetane number mean?","options":["The fuel contains more water, so there is a greater risk of corrosion","The fuel resists ignition, so the engine is much less likely to knock at all","The fuel ignites more readily, giving easier starting and smoother burning","The fuel has a higher boiling point and cannot flow when it is cold"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Cetane is the measure of ignition quality in diesel. Petrol's octane rating works in the opposite way."},
    {"id":"m07-q04","q":"Heavy black smoke from a diesel under load most likely indicates what?","options":["Too much fuel for the air, for example a blocked air filter or a boost leak","Unburnt fuel because the engine has not yet reached its working heat","Engine oil burning past the piston rings and valve seals in the cylinders","Coolant burning in the cylinders because of a failed head gasket seal"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Black smoke is unburnt carbon from too much fuel and too little air."},
    {"id":"m07-q05","q":"White smoke that clears after a minute on a cold start is most likely a sign of what?","options":["A completely blocked fuel filter that is starving the pump of its fuel","A badly worn crankshaft bearing that lets oil into the cylinder","A glow plug problem or cold unburnt fuel that clears as the engine warms","A blown turbocharger that has lost all of its oil in the first minute"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Cold engines often make white smoke. Persistent white smoke with a sweet smell points to coolant."},
    {"id":"m07-q06","q":"Which fuel property shows how easily diesel ignites?","options":["The octane number of the fuel","The cetane number","The sulphur content of the fuel","The flash point of the fuel"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"Cetane measures ignition quality. A higher number means a shorter ignition delay. Octane describes resistance to knock in petrol."},
    {"id":"m07-q07","q":"Why is a direct-injection diesel louder than an indirect one?","options":["It has a much larger exhaust pipe","It uses a spark plug in the cylinder","Its pistons are made of a harder metal","More fuel burns at once"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l1","explain":"With less swirl and a bigger spray, more fuel gathers during the delay and burns at once, causing a sharp pressure rise and the diesel knock."},
    {"id":"m07-q08","q":"What happens during the ignition delay of a diesel engine?","options":["The spark plug fires and the flame spreads","The exhaust valve opens to release the gas","Fuel mixes before it ignites","The piston stops at the top of its stroke"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l2","explain":"The first fuel needs a short time to heat up, vaporise and mix with the air. A longer delay means more fuel burns at once and the knock is louder."},
    {"id":"m07-q09","q":"Retarding the injection timing usually has which effect on emissions?","options":["NOx falls but soot and fuel use rise","Both NOx and soot fall at the same time","NOx rises and soot falls at the same time","Neither of them changes in any way at all"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l2","explain":"Late injection gives lower peak temperature, so less NOx, but poorer mixing and lower efficiency, so more soot and fuel use."},
    {"id":"m07-q10","q":"What limits the amount of fuel a diesel can burn cleanly?","options":["The size of the fuel tank fitted","The octane number of the diesel","The air supply: the smoke limit","The number of spark plugs in the engine"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l2","explain":"Beyond the smoke limit extra fuel cannot find oxygen to burn. It makes black smoke, heat and wear."},
    {"id":"m07-q11","q":"What is the job of the water separator in a diesel fuel filter?","options":["To raise the cetane number of the fuel before it reaches the pump","To add a little water to the fuel so that it cools the combustion chamber","To remove water from the fuel before it reaches the pump and injectors","To warm the fuel in cold weather so that any wax crystals can melt"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Water damages pumps and injectors. The separator holds it in a bowl that you drain."},
    {"id":"m07-q12","q":"After changing a diesel fuel filter, why must you bleed the system?","options":["The new filter is empty of the lubricating oil that it needs to work","The fuel system must be brought up to its working temperature first","Air must be added to the fuel so that it burns more easily","Air in the system stops the pump pushing fuel through the injectors"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Air compresses, so the pump cannot build pressure. Prime until fuel flows without bubbles."},
    {"id":"m07-q13","q":"What is microbial growth in a diesel tank?","options":["Bacteria and fungi that grow where water meets fuel and can block filters","A form of rust that grows slowly on the inside of a steel fuel tank","A type of fuel additive that is used to raise the cetane number","A coating of wax that forms on the fuel filter in very cold weather"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Slime-like growth feeds on the fuel at the water boundary. Keep water out of the tank."},
    {"id":"m07-q14","q":"What is 'waxing' in diesel fuel?","options":["Wax crystals forming in cold weather that can block the filter","Water freezing in the fuel tank when the weather is very hot and dry","A layer of oil on the injectors that builds up after cold starting","The fuel burning with a candle-like flame as it leaves the injector"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Wax crystals appear below a temperature set by the grade of fuel. Winter fuels and heaters help."},
    {"id":"m07-q15","q":"Why should the fuel tank be kept full if possible?","options":["It reduces condensation, so less water forms inside the tank","It increases the cetane number of the fuel inside the tank itself","It makes the fuel burn faster once it has been injected","It stops the engine from needing the lift pump to feed the fuel"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"A full tank leaves little air for moisture to condense from."},
    {"id":"m07-q16","q":"What is the first thing to suspect if a diesel stalls after a filter change?","options":["Air in the fuel system","A flat spare tyre","A low washer fluid level","A faulty rear light"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Air gets in when a filter is changed. Air compresses, so the pump cannot push fuel through the injectors. Bleed the system."},
    {"id":"m07-q17","q":"Why does ultra-low sulphur diesel need a lubricity additive?","options":["The sulphur is replaced by water","The fuel turns into a thick wax","The additive raises the octane number","Sulphur removal loses lubrication"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"The processing that removes sulphur also removes compounds that lubricate pump parts. Additives restore the protection."},
    {"id":"m07-q18","q":"A diesel is stored in a hot damp climate in a half-empty tank. What problem is likely?","options":["The diesel turns into petrol over time","Water and microbes that block filters","The cetane number rises to a very high value","The fuel turns into a solid block of wax"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l3","explain":"Condensation puts water in the tank. Microbes live at the water and fuel boundary and form a slime that blocks filters. Keep the tank full and drain the separator."},
    {"id":"m07-q19","q":"How does a common-rail injector get its fuel?","options":["From a shared high-pressure rail that is kept at a controlled pressure","From the low-pressure lift pump through a plain unpressurised supply line","From a pump that is driven directly by a camshaft lobe above each cylinder","From a separate small pump that is built into each of the cylinder heads"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"The rail acts like an accumulator. The injectors are opened electrically."},
    {"id":"m07-q20","q":"What is the purpose of a pilot injection?","options":["An injection that cleans the nozzle between each of the main injections","A final injection that burns off the soot that has collected in the exhaust","An injection that starts the engine without needing a starter motor at all","A small first injection that softens the start of combustion and reduces noise"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"Pilot injection reduces the sudden pressure rise which causes diesel knock."},
    {"id":"m07-q21","q":"Which is NOT a feature of a common-rail system?","options":["Electrically operated injectors that the computer opens and closes","One pumping element and one mechanical governor for every cylinder","Fuel pressure that is largely independent of engine speed and load","A pressure sensor on the rail that reports the pressure to the computer"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"A separate pump element per cylinder with a governor describes an in-line pump."},
    {"id":"m07-q22","q":"What is the main danger when checking a high-pressure diesel line for leaks?","options":["Fuel spraying at very high pressure can penetrate the skin","The fuel is poisonous when it touches the hot metal of the pipe","The pipe may be hot enough to burn the hand that is held near it","The fuel will freeze solid as soon as it sprays out into the air"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"Never use a hand to check for a leak. Use cardboard, a safe distance and eye protection."},
    {"id":"m07-q23","q":"On a common-rail engine, the rail pressure sensor fails. What is a likely result?","options":["The glow plugs stay on all the time until the engine is switched off","Hard starting or limp mode because the computer cannot control the pressure","The turbocharger spins backwards when the engine is running idle","The engine runs perfectly with no change at all in how it behaves"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"The computer needs the pressure signal to control the pump and injectors."},
    {"id":"m07-q24","q":"What is stored in the rail of a common-rail system?","options":["Diesel fuel at very high pressure","Engine coolant under high pressure","Compressed air for the engine intake","Brake fluid for the brake pedal circuit"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"The rail holds fuel at high pressure, ready for every injector. The computer controls when each injector opens."},
    {"id":"m07-q25","q":"Why does the computer inject a small amount of fuel before the main injection?","options":["It cleans the injector nozzle between the main events","It starts a gentle burn, so it is quieter","It cools the glow plug before the engine is started","It raises the rail pressure to a higher value"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l4","explain":"A small early injection starts combustion softly so that the main injection burns smoothly, reducing the diesel knock."},
    {"id":"m07-q26","q":"What does the helix on an in-line pump plunger control?","options":["The temperature of the fuel","The octane number of the fuel","The speed of the starter motor","The quantity of fuel delivered"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l5","explain":"Turning the plunger changes the point at which the helix opens a port and ends delivery, so it changes the quantity delivered."},
    {"id":"m07-q27","q":"Why is a nozzle tester used with eye protection?","options":["The spray is very cold and can freeze skin","The spray gives off bright light","The spray can penetrate the skin","The tester can shatter into pieces"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l5","explain":"Fuel at injection pressure can penetrate skin and cause serious injury. Keep hands clear of the spray."},
    {"id":"m07-q28","q":"A rotary pump engine will not shut off when the ignition is switched off. What is the likely cause?","options":["A blocked air filter on the intake","A stop solenoid or wiring fault","A low coolant level in the radiator","A flat battery in the engine bay"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l5","explain":"The stop solenoid cuts off the fuel when the ignition is turned off. A stuck solenoid or wiring fault lets fuel flow."},
    {"id":"m07-q29","q":"What do glow plugs do?","options":["They measure the temperature in each cylinder and send it to the computer","They heat the combustion chamber to help a cold diesel engine start","They cool the exhaust gas as it leaves the engine to cut emissions","They ignite the fuel in the cylinder every time that it is injected"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"Cold metal absorbs the heat of compression. A glow plug restores it."},
    {"id":"m07-q30","q":"How can you test a glow plug with a multimeter?","options":["Measure the temperature of the plug body with the meter probe","Measure its resistance. An open circuit means the plug has failed","Measure its voltage while the engine is running and the plug is hot","Hold the meter lead against the plug nut for about a minute"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"A good plug has a low resistance of a few ohms. Infinite resistance means a broken element."},
    {"id":"m07-q31","q":"One of four glow plugs has failed. What might be the symptom?","options":["The engine will not turn over at all on a cold morning, whatever is done","A loud knocking noise that comes from the exhaust pipe when it is hot","No symptoms at all, in any weather, since the other plugs do all the work","Rough running and white smoke for a short time on a cold start"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"A cylinder with no glow plug misfires until the engine warms up."},
    {"id":"m07-q32","q":"Why should starting fluid never be used on an engine with glow plugs?","options":["It raises the cetane number of the fuel so that the engine over-revs","It can ignite violently against the hot plug and cause damage or injury","It cools the plug so much that the engine can then never be started","It contains water that will corrode the plugs and block the cylinders"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"Ether and a hot glow plug are a dangerous combination."},
    {"id":"m07-q33","q":"Why is a cold diesel hard to start?","options":["The fuel turns into a gas in the tank","The spark plug is too cold to fire","The oil pump stops working when cold","Cold metal absorbs heat"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"Heat from compression is lost to the cold walls. Glow plugs and heaters add the heat needed to ignite the fuel."},
    {"id":"m07-q34","q":"A compression ratio of 18:1 raises 20 °C (293 K) air to about what temperature, ignoring heat loss?","options":["About 293 K","About 18 K","About 930 K","About 5,300 K"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"T2 = 293 × 18^0.4 = 293 × 3.18 = 932 K. Real engines reach less because of heat loss, but it is far above diesel's ignition temperature."},
    {"id":"m07-q35","q":"What should never be used to help start a diesel with glow plugs?","options":["Starting fluid (ether)","A fully charged battery","Fuel of the right grade","Fresh engine oil"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l6","explain":"Ether can ignite violently against hot glow plugs, causing damage and injury."},
    {"id":"m07-q36","q":"What does a turbocharger do?","options":["It pumps the engine oil to the main bearings under high pressure","It cools the exhaust gas by adding cold fresh air to the gas stream","It makes the engine breathe less air at high speed to save some fuel","It forces extra air into the engine using the energy of the exhaust gas"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"More air allows more fuel to be burned, and so more power."},
    {"id":"m07-q37","q":"What is the role of a wastegate?","options":["It adds extra fuel to the engine at high speed to help it make boost","It cools the intake air after the compressor and before the engine","It lets exhaust gas bypass the turbine to limit the boost pressure","It stops the exhaust gas from leaving the engine until it is cool enough"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"Without a limit, the boost would rise until it damaged the engine."},
    {"id":"m07-q38","q":"What does an intercooler do?","options":["It cools the engine oil as it flows to the turbocharger bearings","It cools the compressed intake air so it is denser and holds more oxygen","It heats the air so that the fuel in the cylinders burns much faster","It warms the diesel fuel in cold weather to stop wax from forming"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"Cooler air is denser, so more oxygen reaches the cylinders."},
    {"id":"m07-q39","q":"What is the most common cause of turbocharger failure?","options":["Using winter-grade diesel fuel in the cold months of the year","Cleaning the intercooler too often with a mild water-based cleaner","Using the engine at low speed for long periods around the town","Oil starvation or contaminated oil"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"The shaft runs at a very high speed on a film of oil. Find the cause before fitting a new turbo."},
    {"id":"m07-q40","q":"A diesel has low power and a whistling noise at full load. What could be the cause?","options":["A flat battery that cannot turn the starter motor fast enough","A weak wiper motor that draws too much current from the battery","A leak in the boost pipe or intercooler hose","A worn clutch plate in the gearbox that is slipping under load"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"Boost leaks let pressure escape, so the engine gets less air."},
    {"id":"m07-q41","q":"What is variable geometry on a turbocharger?","options":["A second compressor that starts working only at very high engine speed","A turbine wheel that changes its size as the exhaust temperature rises","A bypass valve that vents the surplus boost pressure to the open air","Moving vanes that change the exhaust flow to give boost over a wide speed range"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"The computer moves the vanes to give boost at low and high speeds."},
    {"id":"m07-q42","q":"Why does cooling the compressed intake air help the engine?","options":["Cooler air burns faster and so reduces noise","Cooler air lubricates the turbo bearings better","Cooler air is denser, so more oxygen enters","Cooler air raises the boost pressure much further"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"Compressing air heats it, and hot air is less dense. An intercooler cools it so more oxygen enters the cylinder, and peak temperatures fall."},
    {"id":"m07-q43","q":"Actual boost is 0.9 bar and desired is 1.6 bar. Which test is useful next?","options":["Pressure test the intake for a leak","Replace the injectors without testing","Change the engine oil and the filter","Check the tyre pressures on all wheels"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"A big difference between desired and actual boost points to a boost leak, a stuck actuator or a failed turbo. A leak is the commonest."},
    {"id":"m07-q44","q":"Before fitting a new turbocharger, what must you do?","options":["Run the engine at high revs to bed it in","Remove the oil filter to help oil flow","Fill the intercooler with oil","Find why the old one failed"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l7","explain":"Most turbo failures come from oil starvation, dirty oil or debris. A new turbo will fail again if the cause is not corrected."},
    {"id":"m07-q45","q":"What should you do first if a diesel engine will not start after a filter change?","options":["Fit new glow plugs","Replace the injectors one by one","Prime and bleed the fuel system","Replace the starter motor"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l8","explain":"Air in the system is the most likely cause after a filter change."},
    {"id":"m07-q46","q":"Petrol has been put into a diesel tank by mistake. What is the correct action?","options":["Add engine oil to the tank to protect the pump","Do not start the engine, and drain the tank and lines","Fill the tank to the top with diesel and drive away","Start the engine and run it until the petrol is used up"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l8","explain":"Petrol lacks the lubrication that diesel pump and injectors need. Running the engine can destroy them."},
    {"id":"m07-q47","q":"What does a high injector return flow indicate?","options":["The rail pressure sensor is faulty and is reading too high a value","The fuel has a high cetane number and is burning very cleanly","The injector is worn and leaking internally","The injector is working perfectly and is spraying a fine, even mist"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l8","explain":"Too much fuel is escaping through the injector's control valve."},
    {"id":"m07-q48","q":"A diesel enters limp mode. What does this mean?","options":["The computer detects a fault and restricts engine power to protect it","The engine is out of fuel and will stop in a few more minutes of use","The battery is flat and the starter motor cannot turn the engine","The driver has switched on an economy mode by pressing a button"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l8","explain":"A scan tool will show the fault code that caused it."},
    {"id":"m07-q49","q":"What should you do before beginning work on a diesel fault?","options":["Read the fault codes with a scan tool","Replace the air filter and the fuel filter together","Start the engine and leave it running for an hour","Remove the injectors to check them"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l8","explain":"Fault codes point to the circuit and save time."},
    {"id":"m07-q50","q":"What happens if a new injector is fitted but not coded?","options":["The injector will fit more tightly","The engine may run unevenly","The fuel pressure will rise to double","The engine runs better than before"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l9","explain":"The computer uses the correction code to adjust that injector's quantity. Without it the cylinders are unbalanced."},
    {"id":"m07-q51","q":"Rail pressure at cranking must reach about what value for the engine to start?","options":["About 200 to 300 bar","About 2 bar, as in a petrol system","About 20 bar, as in a mechanical pump","About 5,000 bar, as in a hydraulic press"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l9","explain":"Common-rail injectors need a minimum pressure to open and atomise the fuel. If the pressure stays below this, the engine will crank but not start."},
    {"id":"m07-q52","q":"Metal particles are found in the fuel filter of a common-rail engine. What must be done?","options":["Change the filter only and carry on","Replace filter, lines and failed parts","Add a fuel additive to dissolve it","Ignore it because small particles are normal"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l9","explain":"Metal means a pump or injector is failing and has spread debris through the whole system. If it is not cleaned, new parts are damaged at once."},
    {"id":"m07-q53","q":"Why clean around fuel connections before opening them?","options":["It makes the spanner grip better","It keeps the engine bay looking tidy","It warms the fuel inside the line","Dirt can damage nozzles and pumps"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l9","explain":"Common-rail parts have clearances measured in thousandths of a millimetre. Tiny particles score or block them."},
    {"id":"m07-q54","q":"Why is a petrol compression tester unsuitable for a diesel?","options":["The adapter fits only spark plug holes","It reads in the wrong units for diesel","Its range is too low for diesel","It cannot be used on a cold engine"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l10","explain":"A diesel makes 25 to 40 bar, which is more than a petrol tester is designed for. Use a diesel tester with the right adapter."},
    {"id":"m07-q55","q":"What is the typical compression of a healthy diesel?","options":["About 5 to 8 bar","About 25 to 40 bar","About 100 to 150 bar","About 1 to 2 bar"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l10","explain":"The high compression ratio gives much higher cylinder pressure than a petrol engine, and the cylinders should agree within about 10%."},
    {"id":"m07-q56","q":"A diesel engine runs away and speeds up uncontrollably after ignition off. What stops it?","options":["Switching off the ignition key","Pressing the clutch pedal","Cutting off its air supply","Pulling out the dipstick"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m07-l10","explain":"A runaway diesel is burning its own oil, and the fuel and ignition are not the cause. Only cutting the air stops it. Stay clear of the intake and exhaust."},
    {"id":"m07-q57","q":"What is a common cause of diesel runaway?","options":["A flat battery that will not start it","A weak starter motor that turns slowly","A worn tyre on the front axle of it","A failed turbo seal drawing oil in"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m07-l10","explain":"Oil from the turbo, an overfilled sump or a blocked breather can be drawn into the intake and burn as fuel, so the engine runs out of control."},
    {"id":"m07-q58","q":"Black engine oil soon after a change in a diesel indicates what?","options":["Normal soot loading, not worn-out oil","The oil is much too thin for the engine","The engine has a broken piston ring","The oil has the wrong colour of dye added"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m07-l10","explain":"Diesel soot colours oil quickly. Judge oil condition by the service interval and tests, not colour alone."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m07-c01","front":"How does a diesel engine ignite its fuel?","back":"By compression. The air becomes hot enough to ignite the injected fuel.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c02","front":"What is a typical diesel compression ratio?","back":"About 15:1 up to 23:1.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c03","front":"What does cetane number measure?","back":"How readily diesel fuel ignites.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c04","front":"What does black smoke from a diesel mean?","back":"Too much fuel for the air, such as a blocked air filter or boost leak.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c05","front":"What does blue smoke from a diesel mean?","back":"Engine oil is burning.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c06","front":"Typical compression ratio of a diesel?","back":"About 15:1 to 23:1, much higher than petrol.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c07","front":"Direct versus indirect injection?","back":"Direct sprays into a bowl in the piston: efficient and loud. Indirect sprays into a pre-chamber: quieter and less efficient.","lesson":"m07-l1"},
    {"id":"m07-c08","front":"Four phases of diesel combustion?","back":"Ignition delay, premixed burning, diffusion burning and late burning.","lesson":"m07-l2"},
    {"id":"m07-c09","front":"What is the NOx and soot trade-off?","back":"Early injection raises NOx and lowers soot. Late injection lowers NOx and raises soot.","lesson":"m07-l2"},
    {"id":"m07-c10","front":"What is the smoke limit?","back":"The most fuel that can burn cleanly with the air available. More fuel gives black smoke.","lesson":"m07-l2"},
    {"id":"m07-c11","front":"What does a diesel water separator do?","back":"It removes water from the fuel before it reaches the pump and injectors.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c12","front":"Why bleed a diesel fuel system?","back":"Air stops the pump from pushing fuel through the injectors.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c13","front":"What is waxing?","back":"Wax crystals forming in cold weather that block the filter.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c14","front":"What is microbial growth in diesel?","back":"Bacteria and fungi that grow where water meets fuel and block filters.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c15","front":"What is biodiesel?","back":"A diesel blend made from vegetable oils or fats. It attracts water.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c16","front":"What does ultra-low sulphur diesel need?","back":"A lubricity additive, because removing sulphur also removes natural lubrication.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c17","front":"Order to bleed a diesel system?","back":"Fill the filter, open the bleed screw, pump the primer until clear fuel flows with no bubbles, then close.","lesson":"m07-l3"},
    {"id":"m07-c18","front":"What is a common-rail system?","back":"A high-pressure pump feeds a shared rail, and electric injectors spray fuel as the computer commands.","lesson":"m07-l4"},
    {"id":"m07-c19","front":"What is a pilot injection?","back":"A small first injection that reduces combustion noise.","lesson":"m07-l4"},
    {"id":"m07-c20","front":"How high is common-rail pressure?","back":"Well over 1,000 bar, in modern systems up to about 2,000 bar or more.","lesson":"m07-l4"},
    {"id":"m07-c21","front":"Why must you not check a diesel leak with your hand?","back":"A pinhole spray can inject fuel through the skin.","lesson":"m07-l4"},
    {"id":"m07-c22","front":"Typical injection pressures?","back":"Indirect about 120 to 150 bar. Common rail about 1,350 to 2,500 bar or more.","lesson":"m07-l4"},
    {"id":"m07-c23","front":"What does the helix on an in-line plunger do?","back":"Sets the quantity of fuel delivered as the plunger is turned.","lesson":"m07-l5"},
    {"id":"m07-c24","front":"What is the opening (pop) pressure?","back":"The pressure at which the nozzle needle lifts. Typically 120 to 300 bar, from the manual.","lesson":"m07-l5"},
    {"id":"m07-c25","front":"What do glow plugs do?","back":"They heat the combustion chamber so a cold engine can start.","lesson":"m07-l6"},
    {"id":"m07-c26","front":"How do you test a glow plug?","back":"Measure its resistance. An open circuit means a failed plug.","lesson":"m07-l6"},
    {"id":"m07-c27","front":"Why not use starting fluid on a diesel with glow plugs?","back":"It can ignite violently against the hot plug.","lesson":"m07-l6"},
    {"id":"m07-c28","front":"Formula for temperature after compression?","back":"T2 = T1 × compression ratio^0.4, with temperatures in kelvin.","lesson":"m07-l6"},
    {"id":"m07-c29","front":"Name three cold-start aids on a diesel.","back":"Glow plugs, intake air heater and a block (coolant) heater.","lesson":"m07-l6"},
    {"id":"m07-c30","front":"What does a turbocharger do?","back":"It uses exhaust energy to force extra air into the engine.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c31","front":"What does a wastegate do?","back":"It lets exhaust bypass the turbine to limit boost.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c32","front":"What does an intercooler do?","back":"It cools the compressed air so it is denser.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c33","front":"What is the most common cause of turbo failure?","back":"Oil starvation or contaminated oil.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c34","front":"What causes a whistling noise and low power on a turbo diesel?","back":"A boost leak in a pipe or intercooler.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c35","front":"How do you test for a boost leak?","back":"Inspect pipes and clamps for oil, compare actual and desired boost, and pressure-test the intake at low pressure.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c36","front":"Before fitting a new turbo?","back":"Find the cause of the old one's failure, clean the oil feed and drain, change the oil and prime the turbo.","lesson":"m07-l7"},
    {"id":"m07-c37","front":"What is limp mode?","back":"The computer limits engine power when it detects a fault.","lesson":"m07-l8"},
    {"id":"m07-c38","front":"What do you do if petrol is put in a diesel tank?","back":"Do not start the engine. Drain the tank and lines.","lesson":"m07-l8"},
    {"id":"m07-c39","front":"What does a high injector return flow mean?","back":"The injector is worn and leaking internally.","lesson":"m07-l8"},
    {"id":"m07-c40","front":"What is the first step in diesel diagnosis?","back":"Read the fault codes and ask the customer about the symptoms.","lesson":"m07-l8"},
    {"id":"m07-c41","front":"Common-rail pressure at cranking, idle and full load?","back":"At least 200 to 300 bar to start, 250 to 450 bar at idle and 1,500 to 2,500 bar or more at full load.","lesson":"m07-l9"},
    {"id":"m07-c42","front":"What must be done after fitting a new common-rail injector?","back":"Code it to the engine computer with the scan tool.","lesson":"m07-l9"},
    {"id":"m07-c43","front":"Compression of a healthy diesel?","back":"About 25 to 40 bar, within about 10% across the cylinders. Use a diesel tester.","lesson":"m07-l10"},
    {"id":"m07-c44","front":"What stops a diesel runaway?","back":"Cutting off the air intake. Fuel and ignition cut-off will not stop it.","lesson":"m07-l10"}
  ]
});
